    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler.jpg" alt="Adolf Hitler" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hitler_portrait_crop_(cropped)(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Heinrich Hoffmann, CC BY-SA 3.0 de</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> grew out of the collapse of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weimar-republic">Weimar Republic</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazi-party">National Socialist German Workers’ Party</a> (NSDAP), led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Adolf Hitler</a> since 1921, had failed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beer-hall-putsch">Beer Hall Putsch</a> of 1923 but became the largest party in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> in July 1932, winning 37 per cent of the vote amid the mass unemployment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a>. On 30 January 1933 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-von-hindenburg">Paul von Hindenburg</a> appointed Hitler chancellor at the head of a coalition cabinet in which conservatives such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-von-papen">Franz von Papen</a> expected to control him. Within months the Nazis destroyed the constitutional order. After the Reichstag fire of 27 February the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag-fire-decree">Reichstag Fire Decree</a> suspended civil liberties, and on 23 March the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enabling-act-of-1933">Enabling Act</a> gave the cabinet power to make laws without parliament; only the Social Democrats voted against it, the Communist deputies having already been arrested or driven into hiding. Trade unions were dissolved on 2 May, the state governments were placed under Nazi Reich governors, and by July 1933 the NSDAP was the only legal party. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/night-of-the-long-knives">Night of the Long Knives</a> (30 June–2 July 1934) Hitler had the SA leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ernst-rohm">Ernst Röhm</a> and other rivals murdered, and when Hindenburg died on 2 August 1934 he merged the offices of president and chancellor as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuhrer">Führer</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sinti"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sinti.jpg" alt="Sinti" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sinti">Sinti</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_183-J0525-0500-003,_Rheinland,_Sinti_und_Roma_mit_Wohnwagen_auf_Landstra%C3%9Fe.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">UnknownUnknown, CC BY-SA 3.0 de</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The regime promised a racially defined <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volksgemeinschaft">Volksgemeinschaft</a></em>, or national community, from which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and others deemed alien or unfit were excluded. Jews began to be driven from the civil service in April 1933, stripped of citizenship by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg-laws">Nuremberg Laws</a> of September 1935 and terrorised in the November pogrom of 1938, after which their businesses were confiscated; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Roma</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sinti">Sinti</a>, homosexual men and people labelled ‘asocial’ were also persecuted, and about 70,000 disabled patients were murdered in the Aktion T4 programme by August 1941. Independent organisations were replaced by Nazi bodies such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-labour-front">German Labour Front</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-ley">Robert Ley</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hitler-youth">Hitler Youth</a> and the Strength through Joy leisure organisation. Unemployment, about six million in early 1933, fell to near zero by 1938, driven above all by rearmament financed through deficit spending and the disguised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mefo-bills">Mefo bills</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hjalmar-schacht">Hjalmar Schacht</a>, alongside public works such as the motorways. From 1936 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-goring">Hermann Göring</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-year-plan">Four Year Plan</a> pursued self-sufficiency in fuel, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a>, while consumption was held down. During the war the economy rested on plunder and forced labour: by 1944 some seven million foreign civilian workers and prisoners of war were working in the Reich, many in brutal conditions.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-britain"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-britain.jpg" alt="Battle of Britain" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-britain">Battle of Britain</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spitfire_and_He_111_during_Battle_of_Britain_1940.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Australian armed forces, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Hitler</a> made rearmament the regime’s first priority. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">Conscription</a> was reintroduced in March 1935 in defiance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichswehr">Reichswehr</a> became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wehrmacht">Wehrmacht</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a> was unveiled and troops reoccupied the demilitarised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> in March 1936. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/condor-legion">Condor Legion</a> fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-franco">Franco</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>. After annexing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudetenland">Sudetenland</a> in 1938 and occupying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> in March 1939, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> on 1 September 1939, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> declared war. Fast campaigns combining armoured divisions and air power overran Poland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> and France by June 1940, but the Luftwaffe lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-britain">Battle of Britain</a>. In April 1941 Germany conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, and on 22 June 1941 it launched <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-barbarossa">Operation Barbarossa</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> with some three million German troops. The offensive stalled before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> in December 1941, the month Hitler declared war on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. Defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stalingrad">Battle of Stalingrad</a>, where the Sixth Army surrendered in early 1943, and the failed offensive at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kursk">Battle of Kursk</a> that July lost the initiative in the east. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kriegsmarine">Kriegsmarine</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/u-boat">U-boat</a> campaign was defeated in the Atlantic, and after the Allied landings in Normandy in June 1944 the Wehrmacht was pressed back on every front.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/hermann-goring"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hermann-goring.jpg" alt="Hermann Göring" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/hermann-goring">Hermann Göring</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hermann_Goering_-_Nuremberg_2_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Charles Alexander, Office of the United States Chief of Counsel, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> was a dictatorship built on the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuhrerprinzip">Führerprinzip</a></em>, the principle that all authority flowed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Hitler</a>, whose will stood above law. In practice government was a tangle of overlapping agencies, which historians call polycracy: old ministries coexisted with party offices, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauleiter">Gauleiter</a> who ran regional party districts, and special plenipotentiaries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermann-goring">Göring</a> for the economy and, from 1942, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-speer">Albert Speer</a> for armaments. Rival chiefs competed to anticipate Hitler’s wishes, a dynamic that radicalised policy. The federal states lost their parliaments in 1934, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichstag">Reichstag</a> became a stage for acclamation, and judges applied Nazi principles, with political cases sent to special courts and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-court-germany">People’s Court</a>. Terror was organised by the SS under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heinrich-himmler">Heinrich Himmler</a>, which absorbed the police, ran the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gestapo">Gestapo</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camps</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dachau-concentration-camp">Dachau</a>, opened in March 1933, onwards, and in 1939 combined its security services in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reich-security-main-office">Reich Security Main Office</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reinhard-heydrich">Reinhard Heydrich</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-goebbels">Joseph Goebbels</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reich-ministry-of-public-enlightenment-and-propaganda">Ministry of Propaganda</a> controlled press, radio, film and the arts through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reich-chamber-of-culture">Reich Chamber of Culture</a>. In occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> Germany ruled through annexation, Reich commissariats in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and the Soviet territories, military administrations, and collaborating regimes such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vichy">Vichy</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the Independent State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/belzec-extermination-camp"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/belzec-extermination-camp.jpg" alt="Bełżec extermination camp" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/belzec-extermination-camp">Bełżec extermination camp</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lubelszczyna_marzec_1942_%C5%BBydzi_w_drodze_do_obozu_zag%C5%82ady_w_Be%C5%82%C5%BCcu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">nieznany/unknown, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> caused the most destructive war in history and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>. In occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, mobile killing units of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/einsatzgruppen">Einsatzgruppen</a> shot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in the Soviet territories from 1941, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wannsee-conference">Wannsee Conference</a> of January 1942 coordinated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deportation">deportation</a> of Jews from across the continent to extermination camps in occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chelmno">Chełmno</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belzec-extermination-camp">Bełżec</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sobibor-extermination-camp">Sobibór</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treblinka-extermination-camp">Treblinka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majdanek-concentration-camp">Majdanek</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auschwitz-concentration-camp">Auschwitz-Birkenau</a>. About six million Jews were murdered, together with hundreds of thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Roma</a>, some three million Soviet prisoners of war who died of starvation and abuse, and millions of Polish and Soviet civilians. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> in Europe killed tens of millions. The response to these crimes shaped the post-war order: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> trials established that leaders could be judged for aggressive war, war crimes and crimes against humanity, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide-convention">Genocide Convention</a> of 1948 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/universal-declaration-of-human-rights">Universal Declaration of Human Rights</a> followed. The war left Germany divided, moved Poland’s borders westwards and led to the expulsion of some twelve million Germans from eastern Europe. It brought Soviet power into central Europe and set the stage for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>. In Germany the confrontation with the Nazi past became a lasting theme of politics, law, education and memory, and Nazi symbols remain banned there.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/normandy-landings"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/normandy-landings.jpg" alt="Normandy landings" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/normandy-landings">Normandy landings</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Into_the_Jaws_of_Death_23-0455M_edit.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Chief Photographer's Mate (CPHoM) Robert F. Sargent, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The regime’s end came through total military defeat. From 1943 Allied bombing devastated German cities, killing hundreds of thousands, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-goebbels">Goebbels</a> proclaimed ‘<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/total-war">total war</a>’ in February 1943 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-speer">Speer</a> pushed armaments output to its peak in 1944. The Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-bagration">Operation Bagration</a> in June 1944 destroyed Army Group Centre in the same month as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normandy-landings">Normandy landings</a>. On 20 July 1944 officers around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claus-von-stauffenberg">Claus von Stauffenberg</a> tried to kill <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adolf-hitler">Hitler</a> with a bomb at his East Prussian headquarters; the plot failed and thousands were arrested and many executed. The last great German offensive in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardennes">Ardennes</a> in December 1944 collapsed, and in 1945 Allied armies crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and the Oder. With the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> fighting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, Hitler killed himself in his bunker on 30 April 1945, naming Grand Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-donitz">Karl Dönitz</a> as head of state. German forces surrendered unconditionally in documents signed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> on 7 May and in Berlin on 8 May. The Dönitz government at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flensburg">Flensburg</a> was arrested on 23 May, and on 5 June the four Allied powers assumed supreme authority. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> was divided into occupation zones, its eastern territories were placed under Polish and Soviet administration, and its surviving leaders were tried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> in 1945–1946.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> turned a democratic republic into a racial dictatorship within months, rearmed, conquered most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and murdered six million <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and millions of others in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holocaust">Holocaust</a>; its total defeat in 1945 reshaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-law">international law</a>, divided Germany and Europe, and made its crimes a permanent point of moral reference.</em></p>

